<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brahmanism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brahmanical Hinduism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brahmanism is the name given to the Vedic tradition of religion and social order maintained by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priests, centred on sacrifice, ritual purity, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning and the hierarchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>. In early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> kings such as the Varmans and Senas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> promoted it by granting land to Brahmins and patronising scholars of Vedic ritual and law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brahmanism.jpg" alt="Brahmanism" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Late_Vedic_Culture_(1100-500_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
